## 2. What is genuinely non-obvious in this evidence base

1. **DCA's slot economics set a HIGHER announcement floor than IAD's, not a
   lower one.** Airline strategist (finding 4) and Virtual Chris (dot (c))
   arrive at the same conclusion from different disciplines. A missed slot at
   DCA is a chargeable, unrecoverable event because slots are federally
   scarce and there is no runway alternative — a boarding-call that is
   missed because a passenger was in a "quiet zone" is not the same
   inconvenience it would be at IAD. The $2-CPE-advantage condition on
   MWAA's beyond-perimeter payment stream makes this a dollarized question.
2. **The Berlin Brandenburg failure was a PA-adjacent life-safety
   integration failure, not a general "big-project overrun" story.** The
   2012 cancellation and the subsequent eight-year drag settled on a
   bespoke smoke-extraction and voice-notification system that never passed
   certification against the frozen design. That is the exact class of
   failure a quiet-by-design standard is meant to prevent, because voice
   evacuation, PA zoning, adjacent-zone spillover, and acoustic performance
   all live on the same integrated specification.
3. **"Silent" is a rule about exceptions.** Every peer operator on the run's
   named list — LCY, HEL, AMS, and the newer Chennai and Changi entries —
   keeps gate, disruption, and emergency announcements. The ledger contains
   no primary evidence of a total-silence airport anywhere. The Strategist
   should treat the peer-precedent argument as a case for governance and
   exception discipline, not a case for the absence of PA.
4. **The MWAA seven-volume Airports Authority Design Manual is the actual
   fight surface, and tenant volumes may matter more than the base
   terminal volume.** Airport COO (finding 1) and Virtual Christian (dot
   (b)) converge on the same insight: the acoustic environment on opening
   day is set by what MWAA allows to be added on top of the base shell —
   retail fitouts, concessionaire AV, gate-agent handhelds, tenant TVs. The
   base building spec cannot achieve the standard alone.
5. **The 1:10:100 heuristic is not a proof but it is directionally correct
   for airport PA and acoustics.** ACM cost engineering literature carries
   it; the airport-specific per-square-foot data does not exist in the
   public record. Quantitative Analyst Exhibit E1 uses commercial
   contractor benchmarks ($5–15/sf new; $18–45/sf retrofit) to show a 2×
   to 4× premium for late specification. The direction is safe; the
   magnitude at MWAA would need the authority's own cost history.
6. **The alarm-fatigue analog from hospital medicine is a better template
   than any airport peer.** Virtual Christian is the only voice in the
   swarm advancing this frame, and it explains something no airport peer
   comparison does: why "reduce announcements" as a policy has failed
   everywhere it has been tried without a tiered protocol. Boston Medical
   went from ~1M alarms/week to ~400K by eliminating low-priority
   categories entirely; Johns Hopkins cut 63% of ICU alarms with one
   threshold change. The mechanism transfers.
7. **Airport CEO's naming argument is not cosmetic.** The word "quiet" in
   any public MWAA document creates a headline vulnerability given the July
   2026 joint White House / USDOT / United announcement and the January
   2025 DCA midair still in memory. The internal document should carry
   "Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard." The peer
   evidence supports this — Schiphol and Helsinki brand it as silent
   externally but govern it as an intelligibility standard internally.
8. **The MWAA CPE trajectory is inside the AA– envelope but is spending
   headroom, not carrying it.** Roughly $5.5 B of new debt across 2025–2028,
   adjusted debt per O&D enplanement peaking near $400 in 2028, and a CPE
   trajectory $12.77 → $20.71 by 2030 before the incremental $13–15 B
   Dulles vision is priced. A retrofit-priced quiet standard flows
   entirely into that curve.

