## 3. Where the swarm agrees, and what the Strategist must preserve

### Agreements (near-unanimous across independent briefs)

- **NFPA 72 STI 0.45/0.50 is the mandatory floor.** Every regulatory,
  operations, engineering, safety, EM, and COO brief lands on the same
  number. This is the safest sentence in the deliverable.
- **Design-manual specification is cheaper than change-order retrofit.**
  Infrastructure Economist, Quantitative Analyst, Airport CEO, and Airport
  COO all lead with this. Disagreement is on how much cheaper, not on
  direction.
- **"Quiet" ≠ "silent."** Every brief that engages the peer record —
  Operations Analyst, Technology Scout, Contrarian, Airline Strategist,
  CEO, COO, DPS, EM — reads the peer policies the same way. The Contrarian
  brief argues the standard adds nothing beyond existing constraints; the
  others argue it disciplines those constraints. Both agree the
  peer-airport marketing does not mean silence.
- **IAD and DCA cannot be governed by one prescriptive number.** COO,
  CEO, Airline Strategist, EM, Virtual Chris, Virtual Christian, and
  Virtual Pat all reach this from different angles. The tiered framework
  the run prompt asks for — mandatory / recommended / context-dependent —
  is the only structure the swarm supports.
- **PA-push authority and IROPS/emergency carve-outs are the load-bearing
  governance element.** EM, DPS, COO, and Virtual Chris converge here.
  Any standard that captures the volume budget without carving out
  emergency, security, and operationally directed messages is worse than
  no standard.

### Live tensions the Strategist must preserve, not resolve

- **Contrarian vs. everyone else on whether the standard adds a
  requirement.** The Contrarian argues that NFPA 72 + ADA/ACAA + ACRP 175
  + a competent commissioning spec already require what quiet-by-design
  would require; a new document is a governance surface, not a new
  requirement. The other briefs argue the requirement lives in
  *integration* across those pieces — that is the value the standard
  adds. The Strategist must answer the Contrarian directly.
- **Virtual Christian vs. the Contrarian on accessibility.** The
  Contrarian frames "silent" as a policy that pushes risk onto disabled
  travelers. Virtual Christian argues the same populations are actively
  excluded by the constant-PA status quo. Both cite Part 382. This is a
  genuine substantive disagreement inside a shared regulatory frame.
- **Airline Strategist vs. Airport CEO on where the standard should be
  written.** Airline Strategist says the terms will be contested in the
  Use & Lease MII process; CEO argues that structured as a design-manual
  amendment inside existing consultant fees, it does not trigger MII. The
  gap between them is the MII-language gap named in the airport context
  packet.
- **Infrastructure Economist vs. Contrarian on the peer transferability.**
  The Economist reads LCY/HEL/AMS as evidence that "silence is a downstream
  operating rule that only works when the upstream conditions are
  engineered." The Contrarian reads them as marketing that no operator
  sustains. Both are supported by the same primary sources. The Strategist
  must land somewhere on this axis.

