## 4. The strongest evidence against the thesis

If the Strategist is honest, the anti-thesis is not weak. The best version of
the case against a MWAA quiet-by-design standard, using only ledger evidence:

1. **No peer operator has published measured missed-boarding, missed-
   connection, or IROPS-recovery data on a silent-airport policy.** Operations
   Analyst (finding on HEL) and Airline Strategist (Case: Helsinki) both flag
   this. Passenger self-report from LCY is "favourable"; wayfinding effects at
   HEL are "neutral"; nobody has published an operational outcome number.
   Any recommendation grounded in the peer record is a design-conservatism
   argument, not an operations-outcome argument.
2. **Chennai's 2017 silent-terminal policy triggered a disability-community
   backlash that airport authority officials publicly conceded.** Contrarian
   record `ev-chennai-visual-backlash` (Deccan Chronicle) documents the
   authority admitting "no separate provisions for the visually-challenged
   are available." That is the operational failure mode 14 CFR Part 382 is
   designed to prevent.
3. **MWAA does not own the microphone.** Multiple briefs (Airline Strategist,
   COO, DPS, Virtual Christian) converge: the loudest instrument in an IAD
   hold room during IROPS is the gate agent's handheld on speaker, and the
   airline's boarding-call rights inside the leased envelope are contractual
   under the 2025 Use & Lease. A design-manual standard that does not name
   airline broadcast rights is decorative.
4. **The federal accessibility floor may already be tightening under DOT
   PRM/ACAA rulemaking (14 CFR Part 382 revisions, 2024–2025).** Regulatory
   & Political Analyst flags this in §2.2 — the rule direction is toward
   more real-time accessible information, not less. Any standard that reads
   as reducing information flow will be judged against a moving federal
   baseline.
5. **The Board has not asked for this.** The Airport CEO brief is candid:
   the thesis solves a problem the board has not asked management to
   solve, at the same moment the White House and USDOT have made the
   Dulles program politically visible. A recommendation to write a new
   standard has to survive that opportunity-cost question.
6. **The 1:10:100 heuristic is a heuristic.** Infrastructure Economist
   names this in his caveats. The direction of the cost-of-late-spec
   argument is safe; the magnitude at MWAA is not.

The Strategist should confront items 1–3 in the draft, not around them.
Items 4–6 are legitimate objections that a good draft names and answers.

