## 7. Do-not-claim list

The Strategist should not, in the final draft, assert any of the
following as demonstrated facts:

1. "Silent-airport policies reduce missed boardings" — no published
   measurement supports this.
2. "LCY, HEL, or AMS practice transfers directly to IAD or DCA" — the
   run prompt explicitly forbids this and the evidence does not support
   it.
3. "The MWAA Design Manual currently lacks acoustic performance
   criteria" — the public record is silent, not proven absent.
4. "ACRP Report 175 recommends RT60 < 3.5 s, STI > 0.45, NC-55" as if
   from ACRP 175 itself — that combination is from a vendor-adjacent
   practitioner article. Cite ACRP's actual numbers (STI target 0.50,
   0.46–0.53 acceptable, SNR ≥ 10 dB(A)) or NFPA 72's floor.
5. "A quiet-by-design standard will produce X% dwell-time or Y% concession
   spend uplift" — the ledger's dwell-and-spend claims (Airline
   Strategist finding 8) are supported by peer trade-press but no
   audited measurement.
6. "The 1:10:100 rule is a proven multiplier for airport terminal PA and
   acoustics" — it is a heuristic; use the direction, not the exact
   ratio.
7. "The July 2026 $22.5 B Dulles announcement is a committed program" —
   it is not signed; trade press has flagged the missing budget-by-
   project, gate-count, and schedule detail.
8. "Announcements at silent airports have been eliminated" — every peer
   operator retains gate, disruption, and emergency announcements.
9. "MWAA controls the airline gate PA" — it does not; the 2025 Use &
   Lease grants airlines broadcast rights inside their leased envelope.
10. "The AviaVox 20% boarding-efficiency figure" or "the $2.8 M remediation
    case study" — vendor marketing and an unnamed-source anecdote
    respectively; not citable.

